Task 1: Install Additional IdM Servers in Geographically Separated Locations
Subtask 1.1: Prepare the Environment
First, let's verify our lab environment and prepare all servers for IdM installation.

Step 1: Connect to Primary IdM Server
# Connect to the primary server
ssh root@idm-primary.example.com

# Verify hostname and network configuration
hostnamectl
ip addr show
Step 2: Update System Packages
# Update all packages on the primary server
dnf update -y

# Install required packages
dnf install -y ipa-server ipa-server-dns bind-dyndb-ldap
Step 3: Configure Firewall Rules
# Configure firewall for IdM services
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=freeipa-ldap
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=freeipa-ldaps
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=kerberos
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=kpasswd
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=ntp
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=dns
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=http
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=https
firewall-cmd --reload
Subtask 1.2: Install Primary IdM Server
Step 1: Install Primary IdM Server with Integrated DNS
# Install the primary IdM server
ipa-server-install \
  --realm=EXAMPLE.COM \
  --domain=example.com \
  --ds-password=RedHat123! \
  --admin-password=RedHat123! \
  --hostname=idm-primary.example.com \
  --setup-dns \
  --forwarder=8.8.8.8 \
  --forwarder=8.8.4.4 \
  --reverse-zone=1.168.192.in-addr.arpa. \
  --unattended
Note: This installation process may take 10-15 minutes. The --unattended flag prevents interactive prompts.

Step 2: Verify Primary Server Installation
# Check IdM services status
systemctl status ipa

# Verify Kerberos authentication
kinit admin
klist

# Test web interface accessibility
curl -k https://idm-primary.example.com/ipa/ui/
